    "verdict": "a==c CONFIRMED at the QUADRATURE level: on a controlled curved surface the kernel/ladder h->0 ratio (1.746) equals the exact continuum co-area integral (1.748), and the two-axis-averaged 1/|gradP|-weighted strict scan converges to the SAME co-area (1.74-1.76 as du->0). The naive UNWEIGHTED scan converges to a DIFFERENT limit (~2.07, the arc-length-measure ratio): bias ~0.33 (~19%). KEY: two-axis averaging is essential for the weight to recover co-area. AT THE REE-OPERATOR LEVEL on the usable grids (G<=21, du>=0.4), (c) does NOT yet reproduce the ladder (gap_c=0.145 at G=21, and ~0.28 at coarser G) because (i) quadrature convergence to co-area is slow (~du, needs G~400 for 1% in the ratio), and (ii) the fixed-point composition amplifies residual quadrature error and the weighted operator is mildly non-smooth (does not Newton-nail, F~3e-3). The naive operator bias at G=21 is 0.263. Practical conclusion: the h->0 kernel LADDER is the reliable estimator of the genuine ~0.28 co-area deficit; both strict (h=0) scans are unreliable at achievable resolutions (naive biased low ~0.01-0.13; weighted slow/non-nailing)."
